inase is involved  in the conversion of asparagine  to aspartic acid  and ammonia  and this enzyme is actually important  for  cancer therapeutics  so  it had been identified that several  cancers are unable to produce asparagine  and they really rely on the asparagine  that's circulating within the blood  so if you were able to find a way to  degrade that asparagine in the blood or  convert it to some other compound in the  blood you could then starve the cancer  cells  for this important amino acid that it  needs for growth  and that's what was developed is  this asparaginase is actually  a candidate with broad use  in in various cancers including  acute lymphocytic leukemia which has  about 6 000 new cases per year  however  there are some improvements that need to  be made with this asparagine asb  and a lot of them focus on production so  you need there's a need for improved  production and purification of this  important enzyme and then also to  overcome production toxicity so this  asparaginase  is somewhat toxic to e coli  overproducing it in particular is toxic  okay so the same type of workflow that  we use for cbh1  the first thing that we did was we  inserted the asparaginase gene within to  the e coli genome  then  we designed different libraries against  that base strain to produce edited cell  library we tested them in isolating  isolate measuring asparaginase activity  so that goes into phenotyping workflow  then you can do rapid dna sequencing by  the barcode  um identify those hits and then you  could go through multiple rounds so i'm  only going to show the data from one  round of this asparaginase genome  engineering that we did  and again we we fell back to these two  strategies to generate diversity and  you're going to hear this over and over  again and we use it for many projects  where we wanted to do an unbiased  approach which is genome-wide libraries  and then we also wanted to do a rational  approach which is targeted libraries  targeting genes that we think might be  important  for heterologous protein production in e  coli  so we did a genome-wide knockout library  knocking out every gene in e coli this  was about 4 000 edits contained within  that library  and then we did a genome-wide promoter  insertion library and in this case  excuse me we took a strong synthetic  promoter  um so promoters pictures have a strong  increase in transcription and we  inserted that in front of every gene  in e coli  so those are our unbiased approaches  again just genomic  then we had a targeted library  and the one targeted library i'm going  to talk about is a what we're calling a  cell morphology and protein folding  library  and what was targeted was various  different chaperones  that can have an impact on  the folding of proteins  proteases um that obviously can have an  impact on the expression of the protein  because it can degrade your heterologist  protein can be degraded by host  proteases  and then genes that are involved in  morphology of the cell  and these different gene classes were  targeted with a couple different edits  so a knockout library or knockouts of  those genes  various different promoters that were  targeting those genes as well as well as  truncations of those genes  so those are the two  library approaches again this unbiased  and then a rational approach  so with e coli and depending on what  strategy you're using  you can actually go through this design  generate test learn cycle quite rapidly  and it can be anywhere from two to four  weeks depending on if you're using a  library that might be off the shelf so  like genomic knockout libraries we  routinely have those available so you  can very rapidly take your hit strain  and stack a genome wide knockout library  on them  but anywhere from two to four weeks to  go through this design generate test  learn cycle  for this particular project  somewhat faster than yeast  so i'm showing the um  the primary screening data from these  three different libraries  showing the fold improvement um over the  median which is here at one  we did pretty shallow sampling of only  441 isolates from these three different  library concepts but it was very nice  that they were able to see some hits  that had an even  greater than two-fold improvement  in asparaginase activity  so again  looking back at the cbh1 story looking  at what type of cellular processes  are these genes that we identified as  hits what what cellular processes are  they involved in  we saw five  cell membrane genes  we saw six stress response genes so this  again is not necessarily surprising  because heterogeneous protein production  can be somewhat stressful um to an  organism as well as we know that  asparaginase itself is somewhat toxic to  e coli the cells grow much slower when  they're over expressing asparaginase  we also identified six genes of unknown  functions so we see this repeated theme  over and over again again because we  took these two strategies of rational as  as well as unbiased approaches it  allowed us to pull out these genes of  unknown function that we would have  never identified with a purely  rational strategy  and then we also identified  five genes that are proteases or  chaperones again you know completely  making sense for heterogenous protein  production  okay so as far as conclusions  so the onyx platform really enables  rapid stream development for improved  production for cdmo and cros or for any  other company that wants to develop  production strains  it can increase the yield ultimately of  that product  through various different  library concepts and various different  cellular processes that you might touch  on  that the libraries might touch on  and then also from a manufacturability  standpoint where you can decrease say  product toxicity or overcome  productivity like we did for the  asparaginase project and you can imagine  designing libraries for e coli where  you're trying to decrease the amount of  endotoxins that are being produced  or ultimately end up in the final  product or decrease the  or increase say the tolerance  to phage  or any other  strain property that is important  towards um  scale up to production scale  the platform also allows for rapid  workflows for significant reduction in  both time and resources necessary to  optimize production strains so i talked  about you know for  s cerevisiae for cbh1 it was about six  months to go through three rounds with a  four-fold improvement  and for asparaginase it was about say  four months four weeks or so to go  through this first round and you can do  that in in just a few weeks to go  through these cycles  and it also requires minimal amount of  ftp fte resources because we've really  de-bottled neck the amount of time  spent and and  resources or people resources that are  spent doing the design and build aspects  of these design build test learn cycles  and the ability to sample greater  amounts of diversity genome-wide really  provides more shots on goal in  identifying strains with better  performance  because you can make these very large  libraries very rapidly you have more  opportunities of identifying hit strains  that can have that improved  performance  and also you know one of the issues or  one of the things you care about when  you're going from high throughput  screening small scale  up to  say lab scale fermentations where the  conditions are very different in those  two  at those two different scales the  concern is always do your hit scale up  because the conditions are so different  and because you're able to identify many  hits in this high throughput screening  workflow by building much larger  libraries it gives you more shots on  goal on identifying strains and hits  from your small scale that will  translate into larger scale  fermentations  and with that i'd like to thank you for  your time and attention and i'd be happy  to answer any questions that you might  have  thank you eric for your informative  presentation we will now start the live  q a portion of the webinar if you have a  question you'd like to ask please do so  now just click on the ask a question box  located on the far left of your screen  we'll answer as many of your questions  as we have time for  all right let's get started our first  question here asks can you talk about  generate step more in detail  uh yes so i think um that's in reference  to the actual building of the library  which happens on the instrument  so i can give kind of a brief overview  of what happens  um  on the instrument during the build  process  but i would also encourage you to look  at some really cool videos we have on  our encrypted website there's a nice  video called um onyx instrument tour  that goes through all of the steps in a  little bit more detail  but basically for the for the generate  or what the generate part or what  happens on the instrument is you put  the consumables and reagents onto the  deck along with the cells that you want  to introduce your library in you press  go the door will close  um the instrument takes care of every um  step within the process so it first  grows up your cells  um to a proper od that makes that be  good for um making them competent uh to  do a transformation  um so it grows them up it makes them  competent  for doing the transformation  then it mixes it with the dna that's  necessary to build the library does the  electroporation recovery and then it  goes on to a specialized  microfluidic module where the editing  process actually takes place  then it harvests those cells off of that  module collects it um into your cell  output tube um and then  at the end of the run you take that cell  output tube and then that's what goes  into your phenotyping workflow  that's kind of the basics of the steps  involved in the build that happens on  all the instrument  great thank you  next question here is it challenging to  develop this platform for other  organisms um such as pikia pikia excuse  me  yep  um yep we get that question a lot um  so  as i mentioned you know the the platform  currently supports e coli and s  cerevisiae as the two hosts there's we  definitely hear lots of interest in  other um organisms so pikia is one of um  one of the ones that we hear and um  bacillus is another one that we hear  potential customers interested in  as far as it being challenging i don't  know if i would say it's challenging but  it definitely requires some development  work in order to  optimize the platform  you know there's a lot of work that has  gone into getting optimized design  scores for e coli and for sara vca as  well as um  the the dna architecture that's  necessary um for for getting that  process to take place uh efficiently  within the within the microorganisms so  i don't know if i would say it's  challenging but it does take some  development work um in order to or it  would take development work if you  wanted if we wanted to expand it to  other organisms  great  next question here what was your overall  expression titer of ansb in grams per  liter  yeah that's a good question so we  actually haven't measured um the the  absolute titer um for the for the  asparaginase um  the work that we've done has all been  basically in in small scale  um  high-throughput screening  um so i don't know what  you know what that would translate to if  you went to larger scale fermentation  but we actually we haven't measured um  the absolute titer uh yet  for these strains but good question  great thank you  next question here you said that the  cbh1 gene was optimized for expression  in yeast why do you think you were still  able to identify hits in the alternate  codon library  yes so that was actually an interesting  library so it was based on a um idea  that i had kind of  wanted to i've wanted to test for quite  some time is to look at  you know when we synthesize um code  unoptimized genes is it really the the  most optimum  gene structure for that heterologous  protein  and i think the answer to that question  of why we're still still  able to get hits out is that we don't  fully understand  all of the steps in translation or how  codon usage really translate into  um  translates into the translation of the  ultimate protein so  um  i think one way to think about it is  you know there has been some reports in  the literature that you can actually  over code on optimize a gene meaning  that you can optimize it so that it  expresses so rapidly  that it doesn't have time to properly  fold  and if you  do you know complete codon optimization  that you can actually end up with less  active protein at the end so i think  that's a potential part of it there's  also um  you might imagine that  you might want certain places within the  gene where the ribosome might slow down  or pause for a moment by maybe  introducing a less  a more rare codon at that location and  that will allow for the protein to to  fold or what's been translated so full  so far to fold properly  there can also be differences in in trna  pools at different times during um  fermentation whether it be at the  beginning or the end of fermentation or  in mid-log phase so i think those are  some reasons why um but i think it is it  was very encouraging that we were able  to get some um nice hits out of that  library suggesting that there is still  some improvements you can get as far as  from codon  optimization of the gene  great  next question here do you think the  genome hits identified for both projects  will be generalizable to other proteins  or do you think that they are specific  to those proteins  yeah that's a that's another question  that's good so  the answer is we don't know at this  point  i think it would be really cool to do an  experiment where we test multiple  different genes  multiple different heterogeneous genes  in these different strains to see if we  see an improvement  i mean i can imagine that some of the  hits are probably going to be  generalizable if they're things like  you know overall secretion of any  protein or transcription or translation  or nuclear transport that might be  important  but i could also imagine that there's  certain hits that might be specific to  certain proteins so certain proteins  might have  specific bottlenecks say for proper  folding that other  heterologous proteins might not have so  i i think the answer to that question is  there's probably certain ones that are  generalizable to all proteins and then  there's likely other ones that are  specific um to certain  proteins certain heterologically  expressed proteins  great thank you  next question do you think that after  several rounds of using the first round  hits library and builds you will  eventually stop getting improved strains  yes so um  the answer to that question is yes i  think ultimately once you've  gone through several rounds of this  iterative genome engineering where  you're using the same you know the hits  that you identify from that first round  those different hits  might be  touching on different bottlenecks within  uh multiple pathways that are involved  in heterologist protein production and  once you've kind of combined all those  hits uh into a single strain you  probably um will kind of exhaust all of  the the diversity or the improvements  and diversity that you can get  and that's not necessarily the end of  the project because now what that means  is that there's probably bottlenecks  elsewhere  that you might need to uncover um and  deal with  and what you often do in these types of  um projects where you're doing whether  it's protein engineering or genome  engineering you'll go through a series  of rounds of combining your diversity  into a single strain  and then you want to start adding new  diversity and what you do is you you go  back to one of your say unbiased  libraries like a genome-wide library  genome-wide knockout library and start  adding more diversity again  to your library  and that would allow you to uncover  some new diversity that might have  improved phenotypes  great looks like we have time for one  more question here so we'll wrap up  after this one and this asks instead of  using a single hit strain for the next  round of iterative genome engineering  could you use a pool of strains for  building the next round  yes so the examples that i gave here  we used a single strain that went into  the next round  it is definitely possible to use a pool  of strains so maybe you take five hits  from your first round um and stack say  all the hits on top of that or or  genome-wide knockout on top of that  that's um entirely doable and in fact we  have a an app note um  or a tech note about that um where we  tested that very thing and it definitely  does work  there are some complications as far as  using the software  um the inscripta designer and resolver  software in order to characterize those  hits or those libraries that come out of  it so it's not quote unquote a fully  supported  workflow as far as from the you know end  to end solution that we're providing  with the platform but it's definitely  something that is doable um  on the instrument and we have done that  um and so if you're interested in more  about that i would encourage you to take  a look at that tech note  great well thank you again eric for your  time today and your important research  we would also like to thank lab roots  and our sponsor in scripta for  underwriting today's educational webcast  before we go i'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting questions  questions we did not have time for today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period will be addressed by the speaker  via the contact information you provided  at the time of registration  this webcast can be viewed on demand and  lab routes will alert you via email when  it's available for replay  we encourage you to share that email  with your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event until next time take  care everyone goodbye  you

